[Music]  penny mckinnon didn't set out to conquer  the world of food she began her career  making salads in her tiny home kitchen  and delivering them to happy customers  by bicycle but that led to a  self-published cookbook that became a  cult sensation in her native australia  and then came three more including her  latest that shows us how to turn produce  from our local market into amazing asian  creations  so i mean what i love is like really  focusing on everyday vegetables walk  through hedy mckinnon's local farmers  market what are we looking for we're  looking for broccoli okay and you get a  feast for the eyes now that's a good  piece of broccoli since for the soul  we still have peaches i think i'm gonna  get some peaches and a feeling that even  in the heart of brooklyn you can taste a  bit of her ancestral homeland  this food writer and self-described home  cook wants to show people the basics of  chinese and asian cooking without all  the fuss  it's the basis of her latest cookbook to  asia with love  and the reason we're sharing a seed at  the table when we ate my mum would call  us to the table with the words sick farm  sick fun sick farms eat rice eat rice  it's like that in many asian cultures  the rice is the sacred ingredient on the  table and at her mom's table precision  was key so you have to have a good  command of your chopsticks and we would  eat every single rice kernel before we  left the table so how am i doing you're  doing okay dig in there  michelle  and dig in we did to a traditional  cantonese banquet based on her mother's  recipes and mckinnon's vegetarian habit  some of these things are exactly how my  mum would make like this is actually my  mum's recipe the mapo tofu the vegan  version you know the original version  has the ground pork so my mum actually  started helping me veganize or  vegetarianize  the dishes that she knew i loved the  potato and chive omelette was naturally  vegetarian anyway it was one of her  quick meals growing up and it's so  comforting potatoes and eggs that run  through a lot of cultures and it's like  scrambled eggs and hashbrowns basically  right there is also a japanese-inspired  miso roasted eggplant stir-fried  potatoes with salt and vinegar a nod to  british chips green beans dressed with  fermented soy soybeans my youngest  actually says i want to stop but i can't  when he eats those green beans every  mother's dream exactly  and another asian staple bok choy a dish  i call restaurant greens so yeah  beautiful by the way isn't it beautiful  and my mom taught me how to make it with  she puts a little bit of oil and sugar  in the water that you blanch it and it  just keeps the greenness so the vibrancy  of the color is really important the  other thing about these types of dishes  you know a reflection of me growing up  in two countries and  kind of bringing together those two  influences you know the third culture  that third culture notion came after  what mckinnon calls an identity crisis i  was born in sydney australia i  grew up being very much caught between  the two cultures you know at home my mom  built this very chinese household we  spoke cantonese at home we ate like this  every night we celebrated she lit  incense every morning at her altar to  pay respects to her elders and we  celebrated all the customs but as soon  as i left the front door i wanted to be  australian did you feel that sense of  shame almost yeah i didn't want people  to ever hear me speaking cantonese i  didn't even want to admit that i could  speak a different language until i  started cooking which was really only 10  years ago i really  fell into a career in food and it was  like reclaiming you know who i am  reclaiming my culture and so the recipes  here that you see before you are like me  saying i'm really proud of who i am and  as a mother that i can  bring that to my children mckinnon's  interest in food has taken her through  family community and neighborhood themes  of her popular cookbooks and articles  she's written over the last decade it  even led hong kong restaurant vida to  ask her to create a vegetarian menu they  didn't want a traditional chinese menu  but i got to do things like a soba  noodle salad with my ginger scallion oil  which is what i call my mother source it  was an incredible experience going there  and being asked to do that validating i  would assume i felt like it was impostor  syndrome when i was there because i was  in a kitchen training proper chefs i  don't call myself a chef i'm a home cook  i didn't go to cooking school or  anything but more importantly about that  trip wow it was incredible to go back to  the smells and the sounds that i  remember from childhood which drives her  desire for those outside the culture to  access it especially during a time that  has been fraught with hate crimes  against asian americans i had to go back  within myself and confront those  feelings  of having this book that says love and  then there's there's hate  so it's been a very interesting year to  release that book i could never have  known it resonated with so many people  and people felt comforted by the stories  and to me that's all i want  out of all the food and all the stories  that i create is for people to feel  that comfort yeah this is what i call an  umami bomb it's an umami the kind of  comfort that can only be created around  a table by blending the old with the new  there seems to be a bit of  rebelliousness  in some of these recipes yes  the dumpling chapter it's um i was very  proud of this michelle it's dumplings by  the season so the idea behind it is you  could go to the market whatever  vegetables are in season and you could  turn that into a dumpling anything you  want i was like yeah beetroot that could  go at a dumpling brussels sprouts that  could go on a dumpling i do a lot of  things like that which are not  traditional it is probably  my way of rebelling against the  tradition that my mum presented to me so  it's fun it's you yeah it's me  and i just wanted to show you oh you're  good  [Music]  [Laughter]  i was so impressed  here we go  there we  cheers  [Laughter]  and really she says it's her mission to  like teach folks it's  so easy i mean there are these these  quick and simple recipes that you can  follow that get you to the end result  that a lot of people think oh it's too  meaty asian food is too hard to make  it's so intimidating she says not so  it's the chopstick pea cheer  yeah you like that dude you did wow  very impressed with the chopsticks

